The lowered node ops carry no declaration, so html reads each element's off
the node the op targets. Miss that and a boolean attribute regresses to
setAttribute, which cannot express absence: setAttribute("hidden", false)
renders hidden="false" and the element stays hidden. `hidden` is bound to
state here, so the property assignment has to appear in the handler and in
__sngl_init both.

The elements are named through `sngl:platform/html` directly. Reaching them by
inlining the stdlib wrappers instead is html_element_decl_stdlib.txtar.

deny out/none/html/index.html `setAttribute\("hidden"` -- the boolean is written as a property, since the attribute form has no spelling for false

-- reactive.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:app"
import html "sngl:platform/html"

output { none { html() } }

var shown = false

window("t") {
    html.div(hidden=shown) {
        html.span(id="s")
    }
    html.button(@click { shown = !shown })
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
